# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — no key belongs in this repo.

# ---------- Telegram (required — this is the approval surface) ---------------
# From @BotFather. DM the bot /start once, or it can't message you at all.
TG_BOT_TOKEN=
# Where approval cards go — your own user id (message @userinfobot to get it).
# Required: nothing publishes without a tap, so there has to be somewhere to tap.
STAGING_CHAT_ID=
# Your Telegram user id again. The bot refuses to start without it and ignores
# every message, command and button tap from anyone else.
OWNER_ID=

# UNUSED. Nothing publishes to Telegram any more.
#
# The editorial rule is one destination per kind (src/publish/targets.js):
# a news card is written for a feed and goes to INSTAGRAM; a slideshow is
# written for a vertical scroll and goes to TIKTOK. Sending both kinds to
# several places at once made every account a copy of the others.
#
# Telegram keeps its real job, which never went through this variable: it is
# where cards are APPROVED, on STAGING_CHAT_ID. Setting CHANNEL_ID now does
# nothing at all — no post will route to it — and it is kept only so an
# existing .env does not fail to parse.
#
# The bot refuses to start when NEITHER kind has a destination, i.e. Instagram
# is unconfigured and TikTok is unconnected. A channel no longer satisfies that
# check, because a queue whose only "destination" receives nothing is the same
# silent eating of approved posts the check was written to prevent.
CHANNEL_ID=

# ---------- Drafting (required) ----------------------------------------------
# Unlike BrickDeal's optional title polish, the writing step is the pipeline —
# without a key there is no candidate.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# LEAVE THIS UNSET. It is not "which model to use", it is "use this model for
# EVERYTHING", and everything includes the calls that transliterate a place
# name, parse a slash command or resolve "/trip norway". Those run on Haiku by
# design at a fifth of the price, and setting this line silently cancels the
# whole split in src/models.js - the split is the only reason the bill is what
# it is. Set it only to pin the pipeline to one model for an A/B against a new
# release, and unset it afterwards.
#
# To move ONE tier, use the per-role variables instead, which is what they are
# for: MODEL_EDITORIAL (the post itself), MODEL_JUDGEMENT (place names, region
# hints), MODEL_MECHANICAL (schema-shaped lookups).
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# low | medium | high | xhigh | max. Drafting is a short, well-specified writing
# task; raise it if the Hebrew ever reads flat.
ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=medium

# ---------- Behaviour --------------------------------------------------------
# ONE, not three. A card is one static image and it goes to Instagram alone,
# which makes it the lowest-reach format this pipeline has on the destination
# that matters most. Three a day was most of the account's output; the format is
# good at what it is for and it should not be the account. See src/pipeline.js.
DAILY_TARGET=1            # how many may be awaiting you or approved at once today
# Rejecting a card gives its DAILY_TARGET slot back — a card you turned down is
# not one of "the best two or three a day", and charging the day for it meant
# rejecting the morning's three guaranteed a day with no posts. This is the
# ceiling on how many a day may put in front of you regardless, so the refund
# cannot turn into an endless supply on a day you reject everything.
# Default is DAILY_TARGET x 3.
DAILY_OFFER_CEILING=9
# Ceiling on drafting calls per run — the only step that costs money. Without it
# a day of thin or off-topic sources spends the whole ranked list to produce
# nothing. Default is DAILY_TARGET x 3. /usage in the bot shows what was spent
# and, more usefully, how much of it was thrown away.
MAX_DRAFT_CALLS=9
# How much text a page must carry to be worth a drafting call. Measured, not
# guessed: real sources carried 3,157+ characters, the press-release stubs that
# got drafted and then rejected carried 291 and 346. Two floors because 300
# characters of Japanese is a paragraph and 300 of English is a sentence.
MIN_SOURCE_CHARS=1200
MIN_SOURCE_CHARS_CJK=700
# Lower again for content that arrived in a feed: a feed item carries no menus
# or breadcrumbs, so the same count buys more substance. A full Smithsonian
# volcano report is 779 characters.
MIN_SOURCE_CHARS_FEED=450
# How long headless Chromium stays warm after the last render. It is needed for
# a few seconds a day; holding ~200MB for the other 23 hours on a shared VPS is
# the waste. 0 closes it immediately after every card.
RENDER_IDLE_MS=300000
# The gather runs REPEATEDLY through the day, between RUN_HOUR and
# GATHER_UNTIL_HOUR, every GATHER_EVERY_HOURS — so a source that publishes at
# 14:00 reaches you at 14:00 rather than at 11:00 tomorrow. DAILY_TARGET and
# DAILY_OFFER_CEILING still cap the day, counted in the store, so more looking
# never means more posts.
RUN_HOUR=8                # first hour a gather may run (server local time)
GATHER_UNTIL_HOUR=22      # last hour a gather may start - nothing overnight
GATHER_EVERY_HOURS=2      # how often to look, inside those hours
# SIX HOURS, not four. The drip is one queue shared by every kind, so this is
# the drain rate and the daily budgets are the production rate: at 360 the drip
# publishes 4 a day, which is exactly what 1 card + 1 deck + 2 clips produces.
# Raise a budget past that and the queue grows for ever rather than posting more
#, bot.js warns at boot if the two disagree.
POST_INTERVAL_MINUTES=360 # drip: one approved post every N minutes
SEEN_TTL_DAYS=45          # a source item can come back around after N days

# ---------- Topic mix --------------------------------------------------------
# Enforced arithmetically over a rolling window of what actually published, so
# no single thread can quietly take over the feed. See /mix in the bot.
KOSHER_MAX_SHARE=0.15     # at most ~1 post in 7 carries the kosher/Shabbat tag
PILLAR_MAX_SHARE=0.4      # no single pillar takes more than 40% of the window
QUOTA_WINDOW_DAYS=30
QUOTA_MIN_SAMPLE=8        # below this many published posts the shares are noise

# ---------- Monitoring (Hebrew DMs to STAGING_CHAT_ID) -----------------------
# off | each | digest. Defaults to OFF, and the default is the recommendation: a
# digest every REJECT_DIGEST_HOURS is four messages a day about posts that were
# filtered out, which is four messages a day carrying nothing to act on. What
# was rejected today, and the reason for each, is in /status whenever you want
# it. Turn this to `digest` while tuning the filters, then turn it back.
REJECT_NOTIFY=off
REJECT_DIGEST_HOURS=6
# Alert once if nothing has been staged, or if any single destination has not
# published, for this long. Per destination on purpose: Telegram publishing
# every day while Instagram was blocked at the API is exactly the outage a
# global "did anything publish" check reports as healthy.
QUIET_ALERT_HOURS=30
# Consecutive failures before a destination is called broken: it stops being
# attempted, approved posts are held for it instead of dropped, and you get one
# escalation rather than the same warning on every card. /retry clears it.
TARGET_DEGRADE_AFTER=3
# How long a broken destination is left alone before ONE post is let through to
# test it, and the ceiling that backoff grows to. The degraded flag used to
# clear only via /retry, which turned a thirty-minute outage into however long
# it took someone to notice — and since the alert fires once, on the edge, the
# longer it stayed broken the quieter it got. Set RECOVER_AFTER_MIN to 0 to go
# back to the old latch-until-/retry behaviour.
TARGET_RECOVER_AFTER_MIN=30
TARGET_RECOVER_MAX_MIN=360
# The same, per source feed. A feed that 404s three runs running is stood down
# rather than fetched (and timed out on) every two hours forever. Longer
# cooldowns than a destination: a dead feed is rarely urgent, and there are
# twenty-one of them.
SOURCE_DEGRADE_AFTER=3
SOURCE_RECOVER_AFTER_MIN=360
SOURCE_RECOVER_MAX_MIN=2880

# ---------- Cards ------------------------------------------------------------
# Where rendered JPEGs are written. Leave BOTH of these commented out while
# testing locally — they default to ./out/cards, and the VPS path below would
# otherwise resolve somewhere odd on a dev machine.
#
# On the VPS this points into the directory Caddy already serves, the same way
# BrickDeal's DEALS_PATH points at the web root.
#CARD_OUTPUT_DIR=/var/www/tiyul/cards
# The public HTTPS base for that directory. REQUIRED FOR INSTAGRAM: the Graph API
# takes an image_url that Instagram's own servers fetch themselves, so a card
# that exists only on local disk cannot be published — no matter how correct
# everything else is. Must be https; http is rejected up front.
#CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://tiyul.example.com/cards
#
# Or a LIST, if the same directory is reachable on more than one domain. The
# first entry is what cards are published under; the rest exist so a second
# domain can be verified with TikTok and switched to without a code change.
# Comma or whitespace separated. CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL above is still read and
# appended, so an existing .env keeps working untouched.
#
# Every entry must serve the SAME files — one CARD_OUTPUT_DIR is written, and a
# URL on a host that does not serve that directory is a 404 the publisher
# cannot see coming.
#CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URLS=https://cards.tiyulplus.com/cards, https://cdn.example.com/cards

# ---------- Instagram (the publish target) -----------------------------------
# Official Graph API only. Needs an Instagram Business/Creator account linked to
# a Facebook Page, and an app with instagram_basic + instagram_content_publish +
# pages_read_engagement. IG_ACCESS_TOKEN must be the PAGE token derived from a
# long-lived user token — see SETUP.md §3.3. The wrong one works fine and then
# dies after 60 days.
#
# These three plus CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL are what switch Instagram on, and
# Instagram is where every news card goes. Without them, cards have nowhere to
# publish — and if TikTok is also unconnected, the bot refuses to start.
# CHANNEL_ID is no longer an alternative: nothing publishes to Telegram.
IG_USER_ID=
IG_ACCESS_TOKEN=
GRAPH_API_VERSION=v21.0

# ---------- Instagram auto-reply (optional) ----------------------------------
# Answering a comment with the link, through Meta's "private replies": an
# account may send exactly ONE direct message per comment, within seven days.
# It is the only route from a feed post to a tappable link that Instagram
# offers, which is why it is worth this much setup.
#
# It replaces the plan giveaway. `igReplies.on` in post-config.json is the
# editorial switch; these are the credentials. BOTH of the first two must be
# set or the listener does not start at all — an endpoint that sends DMs must
# never come up unauthenticated.
#
# IG_APP_SECRET            the app secret, from the Meta app dashboard. Every
#                          delivery is HMAC-verified against it.
# IG_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN  any string you choose. Meta echoes it back once,
#                          during the subscription handshake. Put the same
#                          value in the dashboard.
# IG_WEBHOOK_PORT          localhost only; Caddy proxies /ig/webhook to it.
# IG_PAGE_ID               only for IG_AUTH=facebook, where the PAGE sends the
#                          message rather than the Instagram user.
#
# PERMISSIONS. The token needs instagram_business_manage_messages (to send)
# and instagram_business_manage_comments (to receive the webhook and post the
# public reply) on top of what publishing already needs. A token minted before
# those were granted keeps publishing perfectly well and cannot subscribe, and
# the error names a scope rather than the thing it was needed for — so
# regenerate the token AFTER granting them.
#
# Meta's own docs disagree about which of those two the private reply needs;
# the Private Replies page says comments, the Messaging API page says
# messages. Ask for both. Whether Advanced Access is also required to message
# people who have no role on the app is an open question — see DEPLOY.md.
IG_APP_SECRET=
IG_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
IG_WEBHOOK_PORT=8788
IG_PAGE_ID=

# Where the itineraries and the destination pages come from. Only change this
# to point at a staging copy of the site.
TIYULPLUS_BASE=https://www.tiyulplus.com

# ---------- TikTok (optional publish target) ---------------------------------
# Content Posting API, Direct Post, photo posts. The card is published as a
# photo — TikTok fetches it from CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL itself, exactly as
# Instagram does, so that variable is required here too. There is no
# byte-upload path for images.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT FAIL SILENTLY IF YOU SKIP THEM:
#   - The domain in CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL must be verified under URL properties
#     in the developer portal, or every post fails with url_ownership_unverified.
#   - TIKTOK_REDIRECT_URI must match what is registered there character for
#     character, including the trailing slash or its absence.
#
# The tokens themselves are NOT here: `npm run tiktok-token` writes them to
# data/store.json, because the access token lasts ~24h and is refreshed
# constantly — a value in .env would be stale within a day.
TIKTOK_CLIENT_KEY=
TIKTOK_CLIENT_SECRET=
TIKTOK_REDIRECT_URI=

# Shared secret between the website's /tiktok/callback page and this bot.
#
# The callback POSTs the authorization code to the bot as
# `Authorization: Bearer <this>`, and the bot exchanges it for the token pair.
# Both sides must hold the same value; the website's copy lives in its own
# config, not here.
#
# Set it and the connect endpoint starts on 127.0.0.1:8787, which is where
# Caddy proxies cards.tiyulplus.com/tiktok/*. Leave it unset and the endpoint
# does not start at all — an endpoint that writes the publishing token must not
# fall back to comparing against an empty string, and connecting by hand with
# `npm run tiktok-token` still works.
#
# Long and random. Anything memorable is a password:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#TIKTOK_BOT_SECRET=

# Which privacy level the approval message offers first. Only ever used if the
# account actually has it: what is available comes from TikTok per post, and an
# app that has not passed audit is given SELF_ONLY and nothing else. Leave unset
# to default to the most private level available, which is the right way round
# for a default.
#   PUBLIC_TO_EVERYONE | MUTUAL_FOLLOW_FRIENDS | FOLLOWER_OF_CREATOR | SELF_ONLY
#TIKTOK_PRIVACY=

# The domains TikTok has been told you own, under URL properties in the
# developer portal. Checked BEFORE init: an image on a domain that is not
# verified is refused here, by name, rather than handed to TikTok — because
# TikTok's answer to an unverified host is not reliably an error. It can simply
# decline to fetch, which surfaces as a post stuck in PROCESSING and looks like
# nothing at all.
#
# Matched on a domain-label boundary, so verifying tiyulplus.com covers
# cards.tiyulplus.com, and nottiyulplus.com does not pass as tiyulplus.com.
#
# Leave unset and it falls back to the host of the first card base URL — which
# is exactly the behaviour that existed when there was only one host, so an
# untouched .env keeps working. SET IT EXPLICITLY the moment you add a second
# image host, or the second one will be refused (correctly, and loudly).
#TIKTOK_VERIFIED_DOMAINS=tiyulplus.com

# Posts per 24 hours. TikTok's limit for an unaudited client is 5, and this is
# checked from our own published log before calling rather than discovered by
# being refused — being refused costs a post and reads like an outage.
#
# Not bypassable by the owner override, which steps over every guard this
# project invented and none that TikTok did. Raising it past what TikTok
# allows just moves the refusal to their end.
#TIKTOK_DAILY_CAP=5

# The largest share of the published window any ONE PLACE may take. Default 0.2
# — one post in five.
#
# The other quotas cap what a post is FILED as: its tag, its pillar, its source.
# None of them can see where it is about, so a feed can satisfy every one of
# them and still be entirely about a single city — which is exactly what
# happened. Slideshows file as pillar `day` with no source, so the only cap that
# could bite them was the pillar's 40%, and a run of Kyoto cleared it easily.
#
# Compared on the place name alone, case-insensitively. Posts published before
# the field existed count toward nobody's share.
#PLACE_MAX_SHARE=0.2

# ---------- Slideshows (/deck) -----------------------------------------------

# How many deck IDEAS to suggest per day, unasked, the way cards arrive.
# Default 2. Set 0 to go back to /deck-only.
#
# Only the idea is produced on the timer — one model call, no sourcing, no
# renders. Nothing is built until you tap בנה, which is what makes suggesting
# them on a schedule affordable at all: a suggestion is cents, a built deck is
# minutes and a search budget.
# ONE, not two. A deck reaches Instagram as a carousel of stock photographs,
# which is the format Instagram is least willing to show to non-followers, its
# unoriginal-content rule has covered photos and carousels since April 2026 and
# the penalty is exactly that. Worth making; not worth making twice a day.
#DECKS_PER_DAY=1

# And a ceiling on UNANSWERED suggestions, default 3. A week away should not
# come back to fourteen stale ideas — same reasoning as the daily card cap: an
# approval queue you cannot face is one you stop reading.
#DECK_BACKLOG_MAX=3

# ---------- Posts (the five types) -------------------------------------------
# How many of the five post types arrive unasked per day, and how many may be
# waiting for a decision before the timer stops making more. /make ignores both.
#
# THE HIGHEST OF THE FOUR COUNTS, because this is what the account is now: a
# plan, a numbered list, a "stop only flying to X", an honest verdict or a map,
# each built from a tiyulplus.com destination page. The rotation picks the type,
# the look, the aspect ratio and the caption shape, and refuses whatever the last
# few posts used - see posts.* in post-config.json for the weights and for the
# evidence behind them.
#
# Built rather than proposed, like a clip and unlike a deck: a proposal card for
# "a plan post about Prague" asks you to decide on something you cannot see, and
# everything difficult about these formats is visual.
#
# The cost per post is one request to our own API, one Wikimedia call per fifty
# photographs, and a render. The stock image search only runs for places our own
# page has no photograph of, which on the pages checked is about one in six.
#POSTS_PER_DAY=2
#POST_BACKLOG_MAX=2

# ---------- Measuring (section 7) --------------------------------------------
# How often the collector re-reads what the published posts did, in hours, and
# how often the ranking report is sent, in days. Set the report to 0 to silence
# it; /report asks for one at any time and /report fetch collects first.
#
# RE-READ RATHER THAN READ ONCE. A post's figures are still moving a week later -
# most of a carousel's views arrive after the first day and saves keep arriving
# for longer - so a single reading taken the morning after would rank every
# format by how fast it starts rather than by how well it does.
#
# The report ranks on SAVES AND SHARES PER VIEW, never on likes, and it never
# changes a weight: it suggests in words and the edit is made by hand. See
# src/metrics/report.js for why that is a rule.
#
# TIKTOK NUMBERS ARE NOT AVAILABLE and probably cannot be. The Display API's
# video list needs the video.list scope, which this app never requested and which
# a token cannot gain by refreshing - and the endpoint is documented as returning
# videos, while every TikTok post here is a photo carousel. src/metrics/tiktok.js
# has the whole of it, the client is written and ready, and the weekly report
# says which of the two blockers is in the way rather than printing dashes.
#METRICS_EVERY_HOURS=6
#METRICS_REPORT_DAYS=7
#
# Where the numbers live. Its own file rather than data/store.json: that one
# rewrites itself in full on every save and holds the live tokens, and metrics
# grow without bound, are appended to on a timer, and are the one thing here that
# is safe to lose.
#METRICS_PATH=data/metrics.json

# ---------- Clips (short vertical video) -------------------------------------
# How many finished clips arrive unasked per day, and how many may be waiting
# for a decision before the timer stops making more. /clip ignores both.
#
# BUILT rather than proposed, unlike a deck. A deck suggestion is a line of text
# you approve before anything is made; a clip cannot be judged that way, so the
# finished video arrives with approve and reject under it. The cost is bounded:
# the vision judge is capped at clips.search.visionMaxCandidates thumbnails,
# then one writing call and one encode.
#
# Set CLIPS_PER_DAY=0 to turn the daily clip off entirely.
#
# AND IT IS ZERO NOW. Clips average about 117 views against about 415 for the
# slideshows, and the format is honest about why: it is stock footage with one
# line on it, which BRIEF.md rule 4 already calls the weakest thing this account
# posts. The five post types replace the slot.
#
# PAUSED, NOT REMOVED, and the distinction is the whole reason this is a number
# rather than a deletion. The clip code, its two shapes, the vision judge and the
# footage ledger all still work; /clip still builds one on request. Turning the
# daily drip back on is this line, not a revert.
#
# The note below is the previous reasoning, kept because it is still the right
# argument for whatever number this becomes if it comes back.
# TWO, not three, and the reason is that a clip now publishes to BOTH platforms.
# It goes to Instagram as a reel as well as to TikTok (src/publish/targets.js),
# so three a day became three reels on top of everything else, and the point of
# slowing the drip was to put less on Instagram, not to hold the card and the
# deck back while the clip took their slots.
CLIPS_PER_DAY=0
#CLIP_BACKLOG_MAX=3
#
# Where the music beds live, IF you switch the bed on. It is off by default
# (clips.audio.on in post-config.json): the sound is chosen by hand in each app,
# and nothing publishes a reel unattended, so no silent file reaches a feed.
#
# Defaults to assets/audio in the repository, which holds the MANIFEST
# (tracks.json) and no audio: the files are not ours to redistribute, so they
# are dropped on the box and named in the manifest there.
#
# What would bring it back: automating the Instagram half. A reel's audio cannot
# be changed after posting, so anything that publishes one unattended publishes
# whatever the file carries, for ever.
#AUDIO_DIR=/var/www/tiyul/audio
#
# Where clips are written. Defaults to CARD_OUTPUT_DIR, which is the directory
# already served at CARD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL — TikTok fetches video by URL exactly
# as Instagram fetches a card image, so a clip outside that directory has a
# public address that resolves to nothing. Override only for local experiments.
#CLIP_OUT_DIR=
#
# Reasoning effort for the two clip model calls. Both are cheap and neither
# benefits much from more.
#VISION_EFFORT=low
#HOOKS_EFFORT=low
# A deck is built idea-first: Claude proposes what to make, OpenStreetMap and
# Wikidata say which places exist and how well known they are, and then every
# fact that reaches a slide is quoted from the official site of the place or of
# the body that runs it.
#
# Google Programmable Search is what finds that page. Without it the pipeline
# falls back to whatever homepage Wikidata records, and a homepage rarely states
# opening hours — so decks will mostly fail the evidence check. Create a search
# engine at programmablesearchengine.google.com set to "search the entire web"
# (the code restricts every query to one site itself), then take the key from
# the Custom Search JSON API in Google Cloud.
GOOGLE_CSE_KEY=
GOOGLE_CSE_CX=
# Free tier is 100 queries/day and one deck of five costs about five. The bot
# stops at this number rather than letting the quota run out mid-deck.
#GOOGLE_CSE_DAILY=90

# How few slides is still a deck. Below this /deck refuses and reports which
# places were dropped and why, rather than publishing a two-slide list.
#DECK_MIN_SLIDES=3
# Characters before a slide line is rendered one size down. The line is never
# dropped — it was verified against a source, and hiding a verified fact is
# worse than a slightly smaller line.
#DECK_LINE_MAX=26
# Public Overpass is the default and is rate-limited; a private instance or a
# mirror goes here if decks start hitting 429.
#OVERPASS_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter
# Nominatim and Overpass both refuse the default fetch user agent. Identify
# yourself honestly — this is their stated condition of use, not a workaround.
#PLACES_USER_AGENT=tiyul-plus/1.0 (contact: you@example.com)

# ---------- Images -----------------------------------------------------------
# Only three origins are ever permitted: commercial-license stock, our own
# catalogue, or AI-generated. Never an image from a news article or a business's
# own page. Whichever was used is shown in the approval message and printed on
# the card.
#
# Set this and the photo layouts become available - the drafting step is told
# images can be fetched, picks a photo layout when the story warrants one, and
# supplies an English search term. Free key: https://www.pexels.com/api/
# Pexels' licence permits commercial use and requires no attribution; we credit
# the photographer on the card anyway.
PEXELS_API_KEY=
# Leave blank unless implemented - src/images.js refuses rather than publishing
# an image whose provenance it cannot name.
CATALOGUE_DIR=
IMAGE_GEN_API_KEY=

# ---------- Branding ---------------------------------------------------------
BRAND_NAME=טיול+
# Appended to the Instagram caption as a single short line. Blank = no line.
# The caption deliberately carries no source URL (unclickable on Instagram);
# the card's own footer prints the source domain instead.
SITE_URL=https://tiyulplus.com

# Where decks get their places. Our own destination pages are preferred for any
# city they cover: Hebrew names, descriptions written for travellers, a curated
# list and a rating to sort by, all from one request. The OpenStreetMap route
# below is the fallback for everywhere else.
#TIYULPLUS_BASE=https://www.tiyulplus.com

# ---------- Unsplash (the other stock library) -------------------------------
# Tried BEFORE Pexels for slideshows, and the reason is editorial: the Pexels
# travel catalogue leans commissioned and evenly lit, which on a slide reads as
# an advertisement. Unsplash is where people put the photograph they took.
#
# Two obligations come with it, both handled in code: the photographer is
# credited in the deck caption, and a download ping is sent when an image is
# actually used. Leave blank to stay on Pexels alone.
#
# NOT a route to images from anywhere else. A photograph found through an image
# search or a Pinterest board belongs to whoever took it, and a search engine's
# copy of it is not a licence - both libraries here license commercial use.
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=

# ---------- The admin site ---------------------------------------------------
# The same decisions the Telegram bot offers - approve, reject, publish, build -
# in a browser, for the owner and for admins who are not on the Telegram chat.
#
# OFF UNLESS ADMIN_USERS IS SET, and there is no separate flag. An admin site
# with no configured users is a login page with no valid password, which is a
# door rather than a wall; with nobody listed, no listener is opened at all.
#
# ONE PASSWORD PER PERSON, `name:password` separated by commas. Not a shared
# secret: every action is announced in the Telegram chat under the name that
# performed it, and a shared password makes "who approved this" unanswerable.
# It also means removing one person does not mean telling everyone else a new
# password, which is how a password stops being changed at all.
#
# These are plaintext. The file is gitignored and on a box only these people can
# reach, and they guard a posting queue rather than a bank - but do not reuse a
# password from anywhere that matters.
#ADMIN_USERS=neta:choose-something-long,dana:something-else

# Signs the session cookie. Derived from TG_BOT_TOKEN when unset, which is
# already the most sensitive string here and already required for anything to
# run - so there is one fewer secret to lose. Set it to invalidate every session
# at once.
#ADMIN_SECRET=

# How long a login lasts. A restart does not end it: the token is signed rather
# than stored, so a deploy is not a logout.
#ADMIN_SESSION_HOURS=72

# WHERE IT LISTENS. 127.0.0.1 on purpose - Caddy terminates TLS and proxies to
# it, exactly as it already does for cards.tiyulplus.com. Never bind this to a
# public address: there is no TLS here and the cookie would travel in clear.
#
#   admin.tiyulplus.com {
#       reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787
#   }
#
# That block goes in the SAME /etc/caddy/Caddyfile the cards host lives in - see
# DEPLOY.md. Then `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` and reload.
#ADMIN_PORT=8787
#ADMIN_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Only for running it over plain http on your own machine. The session cookie is
# Secure, so a browser never sends it back over http - which looks exactly like
# the site rejecting a correct password. `npm run admin-lab` sets this itself.
#ADMIN_INSECURE_COOKIES=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Narration, for /guide
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The narrated guide format reads a short script over a run of the destination's
# own photographs and burns the words on in time with the voice. WITHOUT A KEY IT
# STILL BUILDS: the same cuts, the same subtitles, no voice. The approval card
# says which one it made, so a silent video never looks like a bug.
#
# ElevenLabs rather than a cheaper provider, for one reason that is not quality:
# its with-timestamps endpoint returns the start and end of every CHARACTER it
# spoke, and that is what makes the subtitles follow the voice exactly. Every
# other provider would need a second pass (Whisper) or an estimate that visibly
# drifts within two sentences. See src/video/speech.js.
#
# Roughly $5/month covers about a hundred of these at forty seconds each.
#ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# WHICH VOICE READS THESE IS AN EDITORIAL DECISION and there is deliberately no
# default. It is the account's narrator; picking one in code would make that
# choice invisible. Browse voices at elevenlabs.io/voice-library, filter for
# Hebrew, and paste the id here.
#ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=

# eleven_multilingual_v2 by default, which is the cheapest model that speaks
# Hebrew at all - the monolingual models read Hebrew text as English phonemes.
#ELEVENLABS_MODEL=eleven_multilingual_v2

# How many postcard reels a day. These are the counted "4 places that look like a
# painting" videos, built from the destination page's own photographs rather than
# from stock, which is what lets them name the place on screen honestly.
# Defaults to 1. The three stock clip shapes stay at CLIPS_PER_DAY, which is 0.
#POSTCARDS_PER_DAY=1
